      <reaction compartment="compartment_70101" fast="false" id="reaction_9026912" metaid="metaid_1882" name="GGT hydrolyses PCTR1 to PCTR2" reversible="false">
        <notes>
          <p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">In human macrophages, protectin conjugate in tissue regeneration 1 (PCTR1) is proposed to be hydrolysed to PCTR2 (16-cysteinylglycinyl, 17-hydroxy-docosahexaenoic acid) by the actions of a glutathione transferase (GGT). Human macrophages incubated with E. coli and a GGT inhibitor led to increased levels of PCTR1 and decreased levels of PCTR3, suggesting a role for GGT enzyme in PCTR2 and PCTR3 biosynthesis (Dalli et al. 2015).</p>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
          <rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:vCard="http://www.w3.org/2001/vcard-rdf/3.0#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/" xmlns:bqbiol="http://biomodels.net/biology-qualifiers/">
