      <reaction compartment="compartment_12045" fast="false" id="reaction_5696119" metaid="metaid_2042" name="AGMO cleaves alkylglycerol into fatty aldehyde and glycerol" reversible="false">
        <notes>
          <p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Ether lipids (alkylglycerols, glyceryl ethers) are essential components of brain membranes, protect the eye from cataract, mediate signalling processes and are required for spermatogenesis. Alkylglycerol monooxygenase (AGMO) is a tetrahydrobiopterin-dependent protein and is the only enzyme known to cleave the ether bond of alkylglycerols and lyso-alkylglycerol phospholipids into fatty aldehydes and glycerol derivatives (Watschinger et al. 2010).</p>
        </notes>
