      <reaction compartment="compartment_161320" fast="false" id="reaction_1605736" metaid="metaid_2115" name="GLA hydrolyzes PSAP(195-273):Gb3Cer:PE" reversible="false">
        <notes>
          <p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Alpha-galactosidase A (GLA) (Bishop et al. 1986) removes the terminal galactose residue from glycolipids or glycoproteins, mobilized by PSAP(195-273) (Saposin B), resulting in galactose and an alcohol. An example is the Fabry disease substrate globotriaosylceramide (Gb3Cer) which is hydrolysed to form galactose and lactosylceramide. GLA functions as a homodimer (Garman and Garboczi 2004) and defects in this enzyme lead to Fabry disease (FD) (MIM:301500), a rare X-linked sphingolipidosis disease where glycolipids such as Gb3Cer accumulate in many tissues (Garman and Garboczi 2004, Eng et al. 1993, Shabeer et al. 2006). Researchers also observed elevated Gb3Cer in prosaposin deficiency (PSAPD, MIM: 611721) cases, a rare disease with low levels of all saposins. Saposin B (PSAP(195-273)) is an essential cofactor to the reaction (Bradova et al., 1993).</p>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
          <rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:vCard="http://www.w3.org/2001/vcard-rdf/3.0#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/" xmlns:bqbiol="http://biomodels.net/biology-qualifiers/">
