      <reaction compartment="compartment_70101" fast="false" id="reaction_8955794" metaid="metaid_2189" name="PGP:Mg2+ dimer hydrolyses G3P to glycerol" reversible="false">
        <notes>
          <p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Cytosolic PGP (Glycerol-3-phosphate phosphatase) catalyzes the hydrolysis of G3P (glycerol 3-phosphate) to glycerol and phosphate. This reaction is inferred from the properties of the human enzyme overexpressed in mouse cells (Mugabo et al. 2016). The enzyme is inferred to function as a homodimer with one Mg++ ion bound to each subunit, from the properties of the mouse enzyme (Seifried et al. 2014). Al-Maas et al. (2022a,b) hypothesize that PGP, by dissipating glucose-derived glycerol in the liver and pancreas, may play a role in minimizing the effects of hyperglycemia. In the male gonad, PGP activity may be required for normal spermatogenesis (Oppong et al. 2024).</p>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
          <rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:vCard="http://www.w3.org/2001/vcard-rdf/3.0#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/" xmlns:bqmodel="http://biomodels.net/model-qualifiers/" xmlns:bqbiol="http://biomodels.net/biology-qualifiers/">
