      <reaction compartment="compartment_20699" fast="false" id="reaction_9844860" metaid="metaid_2366" name="ST3GAL5 transfers NeuNAc to ceramides" reversible="false">
        <notes>
          <p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Following its biosynthesis, a fraction of GalCer reaches the lumen of the Golgi and is reacted with cytidine-5′-monophospho-N-acetylneuraminic acid by the action of the sialyltransferase ST3GAL5 to form N-acetylneuraminyl-GalCer (GM4; sialyl-GalCer) (Shanker and Pieringer, 1983; Ishii et al., 1998; reviewed by Liu and Li, 2013). ST3GAL5 also catalyzes the sialylation of lactosylceramide (LacCer) to ganglioside GM3 and the sialylation of gangliosides GA1/GA2 to gangliosides GM1/GM2. The process is localized in the Golgi membrane (Allende et al., 2000; Stern et al., 2000; Berselli et al., 2006). Deficiencies in ST3GAL5 are the cause of "Salt and Pepper" developmental regression syndrome (SPDRS, MIM:609056; Aoki et al., 2019; reviewed by Inamori and Inokuchi, 2022).</p>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
          <rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:vCard="http://www.w3.org/2001/vcard-rdf/3.0#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/" xmlns:bqbiol="http://biomodels.net/biology-qualifiers/">
