      <reaction compartment="compartment_70101" fast="false" id="reaction_428231" metaid="metaid_2581" name="ACER1 hydrolyzes ceramide (endoplasmic reticulum)" reversible="false">
        <notes>
          <p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">ACER1 (alkaline ceramidase 1) catalyzes the reversible hydrolysis of ceramide to yield a free fatty acid and sphingosine (Sun et al. 2008). ACER1 hydrolyses C20:0-C24:0 ceramides, localizes to the endoplasmic reticulum membrane, and is mainly expressed in skin tissue (Casasampere et al., 2015). ACER1 is believed to be a key regulator of keratinocyte differentiation and has a fundamental role in skin barrier formation (Liakath-Ali et al., 2016; reviewed by Mao and Obeid, 2008; Parveen et al., 2019; Duarte et al., 2020).</p>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
          <rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:vCard="http://www.w3.org/2001/vcard-rdf/3.0#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/" xmlns:bqbiol="http://biomodels.net/biology-qualifiers/">
