      <reaction compartment="compartment_161320" fast="false" id="reaction_1606602" metaid="metaid_2623" name="ASAH1 hydrolyzes ceramide" reversible="false">
        <notes>
          <p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Acid ceramidase (ASAH1) is a lysosomal enzyme that catalyses the hydrolysis of ceramide to sphingosine and free fatty acid. It functions as a heterodimer of one alpha and one beta subunit (Bernardo et al. 1995; Gebai et al, 2018). The reaction is stimulated by Saposin D (Sap D, PSAP(405-486)) (Tatti et al, 1999). Defects in ASAH1 are the cause of Farber lipogranulomatosis (FL) (MIM:228000), also called Farber disease (FD) (Zhang et al. 2000, Koch et al. 1996).</p>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
          <rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:vCard="http://www.w3.org/2001/vcard-rdf/3.0#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/" xmlns:bqbiol="http://biomodels.net/biology-qualifiers/">
