      <reaction compartment="compartment_984" fast="false" id="reaction_8952251" metaid="metaid_2658" name="PLA2G15 hydrolyses LPC to GPCho and LCFA(-)" reversible="false">
        <notes>
          <p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Lecithin cholesterol acyltransferase (LCAT) is a key enzyme in the esterification of plasma cholesterol. Group XV phospholipase A2 (PLA2G15 aka LCAT-like lysophospholipase, LLPL or lysosomal phospholipase A2, LPLA2) bears 49% sequence similarity to LCAT (Taniyama et al. 1999) and is present in plasma. PLA2G15 possesses both calcium-independent phospholipase A(2) and transacylase activities (Abe and Shayman 1998) and could hydrolyse lysophosphatidylcholine (lysoPC), a proatherogenic lipid, to glycerophosphorylcholine (GPCho) and a free fatty acid anion (LCFA(-)) (Taniyama et al. 1999, Hiraoka et al. 2002).</p>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
          <rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:vCard="http://www.w3.org/2001/vcard-rdf/3.0#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/" xmlns:bqbiol="http://biomodels.net/biology-qualifiers/">
