      <reaction compartment="compartment_70101" fast="false" id="reaction_9020257" metaid="metaid_2708" name="LTA4H:Zn2+ hydrolyses 17R(16)-epoxy-DHA to AT-(N)PD1" reversible="false">
        <notes>
          <p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Leukotriene A4 hydrolase (LTA4H) is a monomeric, soluble enzyme that uses a Zn2+ cofactor to catalyse the hydrolysis of the allylic epoxide leukotriene A4 (LTA4) (McGee and Fitzpatrick 1985). LTA4H may also be able to catalyse the hydrolysis of 17R(6)-epoxy-docosahexaenoic acid (17R(16)-epoxy-DHA) to 10(R),17(R)-dihydroxydocosa-4Z,7Z,11E,13E,15Z,19Z-hexaenoic acid (aspirin-triggered (neuro)protectin D1, AT-(N)PD1) (Serhan et al. 2011). In human cells, AT-(N)PD1 decreased PMN migration as well as enhancing efferocytosis of apoptotic human PMN by macrophages, indicating AT-(N)PD1 as a potent anti-inflammatory proresolving molecule (Serhan et al. 2011).</p>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
          <rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:vCard="http://www.w3.org/2001/vcard-rdf/3.0#" xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/" xmlns:bqbiol="http://biomodels.net/biology-qualifiers/">
