      <reaction compartment="compartment_70101" fast="false" id="reaction_9018862" metaid="metaid_2781" name="LTA4H:Zn2+ hydrolyses 5S,6S-epoxy-18(S)-HEPE to 18(S)-RvE1" reversible="false">
        <notes>
          <p xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Leukotriene A4 hydrolase (LTA4H) is a monomeric, soluble enzyme that uses a Zn2+ cofactor to catalyse the hydrolysis of the allylic epoxide leukotriene A4 (LTA4) (McGee and Fitzpatrick 1985). LTA4H can also catalyse the hydrolysis of 5S,6S-epoxy-18(S)-HEPE to the 18(S) stereoisomer of resolvin E1 (18(S)-RvE1) (Oh et al. 2011). The E-resolvins are anti-inflammatory, pro-resolving, and non-phlogistic (that is, they mediate the clearance of leukocytes without eliciting an inflammatory response) (Serhan et al. 2008).</p>
        </notes>
        <annotation>
